 ## Background
-Distance fields have been a cornerstone of implicit surface representation since the introduction of Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) for ray marching [G]. The specific techniques detailed in this family, such as bounded texture sampling and probabilistic outputs, emerged from the need to handle discrete data structures (textures, point clouds) and differentiable rendering pipelines in modern graphics [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. The use of distance fields for haptic feedback dates back to early virtual reality research, where stable collision detection was required for real-time force feedback [S1, unofficial]. The integration of distance fields into neural rendering (PDDFs) and avatar synthesis (Signed Height Indicators) represents a more recent development, leveraging the differentiability of neural networks to overcome the discontinuities inherent in traditional geometric representations [S2, S4, unofficial].
